Z||000006||EdsB52||08-21-2001||12:45 AM||||Didn't we answer this one a couple days ago? Anyway, probability #1 is a bent clutch pedal. Possibility #1 is bent fingers on the pressure plate. I bought an 84 with a fairly new clutch in it that wouldn't disengage. Took it apart and found they had rebuilt the pressure plate by using heat. The heat weakened the spring action in some of the pressure plate fingers causing the pressure plate to disengage only on one side and not fully disengaing. Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000007||stsmithgt||08-21-2001||10:19 AM||||If I remember, hopefully today, I'll take some pictures of what some parts of the clutch are NOT supposed to look like and it wasn't due to abusive driving.  I baby my car except I will put it in a corner pretty fast. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jimmy||08-21-2001||02:06 PM||||TO bearings look lopsided a lot of the time.  This is normal, as long as the bearing spins without any noticable bearing wear its fine.<P>Jim<BR>87 GT<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8||||reg||1
Z||000009||LivoniaFieroGT||08-21-2001||02:18 PM||||Seriously check out the pressure plate fingers.  I had one I replaced with a rebuilt and had to take it apart again due to an ever so slight crack in the PP.  Look your PP over very close. Never ever take this unit apart and think of saving a few bucks. Put in a another PP everytime.<BR>Rob||||reg||1
